# OpenRouter key. Never exposed to the browser - only the server reads it.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=your_openrouter_key_here

# Optional: several keys, comma-separated. The free tier allows 50 requests per
# day PER KEY, and when that window is spent every model answers 429 until it
# resets - hours later. With more than one key the server rotates automatically:
# on 401/403 it drops the key, on a key-wide daily limit it parks it until the
# reset and continues with the next one. `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` above is treated
# as a list of one, so a single key keeps working exactly as before.
# OPENROUTER_API_KEYS=sk-or-v1-...,sk-or-v1-...,sk-or-v1-...

# Preferred model. Optional: the server discovers the free catalog on its own
# and falls back to any working model if this one is unavailable.
# Free is decided by price, not by the `:free` suffix - a zero-priced preview
# without the suffix is just as eligible. Tried first when set.
# Pick one that answers without a long chain of thought first: a reasoning-first
# model can spend 13-17s before the first content token, which reads as a hang.
# Measured 2026-09: this one produced its first token in 4.1s.
OPENROUTER_MODEL=dots-studio/dots-3-note-preview:free

# Optional hard allowlist. When set, only these models are used.
# OPENROUTER_MODELS=z-ai/glm-5.2:free,inclusionai/ling-3.0-flash-fin:free

# Optional blocklist. Useful for free models that only emit reasoning and never
# produce an answer, which the chat treats as unusable.
# OPENROUTER_MODEL_BLOCKLIST=qwen/qwen3.8-27b:free

# Port for the API server.
PORT=3001

# Outbound HTTP proxy for the server's calls to openrouter.ai. On by default,
# not commented out: OpenRouter closed access for Russian accounts and addresses
# on 27 June 2026, and the failure that produces is a 403 from an edge in front
# of the API, which reads like a broken key rather than a blocked address. The
# machine this is most likely to run on cannot reach OpenRouter without one, and
# nobody should have to work that out from a status code.
#
# The port is the local HTTP port of a VPN client - 10809 for v2rayN, 7890 for
# clash, 2080 for sing-box. Nothing is assumed about it: the port is checked with
# a TCP connect at startup, and if nothing is listening the server says so in one
# line and goes straight to OpenRouter instead. A machine without a VPN client
# therefore behaves exactly as it did before, and a machine with one on the usual
# port works without editing this file. To force a direct connection, leave the
# value empty.
OUTBOUND_PROXY=http://127.0.0.1:10809

# Optional. Where OpenRouter is reached from. Point it at the relay in `worker/`
# to route upstream calls through a Cloudflare Worker instead of talking to
# openrouter.ai directly. The trailing /api/v1 is not included: the relay owns
# that part.
#
# Read the README section "Если openrouter.ai недоступен" before using this: the
# relay was deployed and measured, and OpenRouter refuses requests coming from
# Cloudflare's network, so this path does not currently work. It is left here
# because the relay is a working reverse proxy and a non-Cloudflare host is the
# open question, not because it solves the Russian block.
# OPENROUTER_BASE_URL=https://saldo-openrouter-relay.<account>.workers.dev

# Optional. The relay's shared secret. Must match the Worker's RELAY_TOKEN
# (`npx wrangler secret put RELAY_TOKEN`). Leave empty for a direct connection.
# OPENROUTER_RELAY_TOKEN=

# Optional. How many free models the server probes at startup, how many at
# once, and how long it stops probing after upstream reports a spent daily
# window. A probe is a real request, so the count is small on purpose: three
# probes out of a budget of 50 per day. A probe result stays trusted for six
# hours, so re-reading the catalog does not start a new round of probes.
# PROBE_LIMIT=3
# PROBE_CONCURRENCY=4
# PROBE_BACKOFF_MS=600000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything below has a working default and exists so the behaviour is
# tunable without a rebuild. The reasoning behind each number is in
# `server/src/config.ts`, next to the field it sets. Listed here so the knobs
# are discoverable from the one file a reader opens first.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Timeouts, in milliseconds.
#   UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT_MS      one upstream call, start to finish        (60000)
#   FIRST_TOKEN_TIMEOUT_MS   how long to wait for the first content
#                            token before failing over to the next model
#                            - free "pro" models can otherwise leave the
#                            user watching a typing indicator for a minute (30000)
#   PROBE_TIMEOUT_MS         one model probe                           (12000)
#   PROXY_CHECK_TIMEOUT_MS   the startup TCP check on OUTBOUND_PROXY   (1500)
#   DISCOVERY_DEADLINE_MS    hard ceiling on one catalog read; the backstop
#                            that guarantees the shared discovery promise
#                            always settles                          (20000)
# UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT_MS=60000
# FIRST_TOKEN_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
# PROBE_TIMEOUT_MS=12000
# PROXY_CHECK_TIMEOUT_MS=1500
# DISCOVERY_DEADLINE_MS=20000

# Model registry.
#   REGISTRY_TTL_MS     how long the discovered catalog stays fresh  (600000)
#   MODEL_COOLDOWN_MS   how long a model that answered 429/5xx is
#                       skipped before it is tried again              (60000)
# PROBE_MAX_TOKENS=24
# PROBE_SPACING_MS=0
# REGISTRY_TTL_MS=600000
# MODEL_COOLDOWN_MS=60000

# Request limits. Hitting one is a `BAD_REQUEST`, not a silent truncation.
# RATE_LIMIT_MAX is per window per client; the window is in milliseconds.
# MAX_MESSAGES=60
# MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS=24000
# RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000
# RATE_LIMIT_MAX=30

# Comma-separated list of origins allowed to call the API from a browser.
# Empty means same-origin only, which is what the bundled client needs.
# CORS_ORIGIN=https://example.com
